{"vote":"left","reasoning":"Both videos hit every beat in the same order: hook, terminal proof, the Hello, Montagent result, the claim, then the brand lockup. LEFT tells the story more completely. It shows the prompt itself ("You ask. … Add a subtitle under"), then "It edits the project file", then "It renders the video." with the result labelled out/hello-text.mp4. That makes it obvious the picture came out of the session. Its claim ("Now your video is a file your agent can read, check and change.") is set large and bold, stays on screen across two samples, and puts the accent on one word only. The hook also uses the accent on a single word ("video"). The cream ground with the Montagent lockup and tagline is a clean ending.

RIGHT has slightly larger terminal panels and a bigger hook. But its claim is set small and light, and in one sample the accent on "change." disappears. Its end card repeats the claim as a tagline, which adds nothing, and it spends two samples on the result, one of them in a muddy grey transition. Its proof never shows the user's request, so the cause-and-effect story is weaker.

Both look polished and neither shows clear defects in the stills. LEFT wins on narrative clarity and on how readable and confident the claim is."}

p033.raw:
{"vote":"right","reasoning":"The two pieces are very much alike. Both use the same study set and keep the owl on the floor at a good size. Both have a questioning arm-spread early, a laptop with code plus a "Hello, Montagent" video card that pops out above it, a pointing gesture, and an ending with the Montagent lockup next to Hoot. Neither shows visible rig breaks, distortion or corner labels.

The deciding difference is how long the answer illustration lasts. In the left video, the laptop and rendered card are already gone at 5.625 s. Hoot stands alone in an empty study, probably while the answer sentence is still playing. That leaves a blank stretch before the brand appears. In the right video, the laptop stays on screen through 5.625 s, with a progress bar and an upward arrow leading to the rendered video card. This makes "edit the file → it renders again" clearer and easier to follow, and it moves straight into the brand.

The left video does gesture earlier, at 0.625 s, and its 1.875 s frame shows a blink. The right video also shows varied poses and gestures toward the laptop. On balance, the right video delivers the answer beat more completely and has no dead frame."}

p039.raw:
{"vote":"right","reasoning":"I prefer the right video, mainly because it shows the answer beat more clearly. On the right, the laptop sits on a table and its screen shows a visible edit: a word changes from "a draft" to "a document", highlighted in red. A rendered video frame ("Hello, Montagent") then appears, with an arrow from the laptop to it, so you can tell the video comes from the edited file. Hoot gestures towards the laptop throughout. The right owl is also larger and more clearly the main subject, and probably meets the half-frame height rule. The ending is strong: the Montagent lockup, a "Edit the file. Render it again." tagline and Hoot are on screen together over the last two stills.

The left video has the same overall structure: Hoot gestures, a laptop shows code, a "Hello, Montagent" frame appears above it, and the brand comes in. But its edit is only generic code, with no visible word change. The rendered frame just appears above the laptop, with nothing linking the two. The owl is smaller, looks less than half the frame tall, and the composition feels emptier.

One weakness on the right: Hoot starts at the left edge in the first still, probably entering the scene. That is minor next to the clearer illustration of the idea."}
